Z||000000||Richard Parnell||06-16-2000||08:17 PM||||My car is not running right now so I have been borrowing my wife's car and my sons car alternately.  My son has a ricer (he cracks up when I use that term) Integra.  I was amazed at how fast it is especially when you hit about 6500 rpm. it just takes off like another 4 cylinders have cut in. Is this technology being used in American cars specifically V8s?  I would think that it would open up a whole new meaning to the word acceleration.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Eric||06-16-2000||08:29 PM||||A lot of new cars are using this variable valve timing and lift.  It's employed mostly on V6s and 4 cyliders, but some V8s have it as well.  While it increases top end horsepower, there is still no torque to these small displacement motors.  You probably noticed you have to wind it all the way up for the afterburners to kick in.  In the meantime, I've already pulled two car lengths on your son's little rice mobile.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||06-16-2000||08:42 PM||||You mean VTEC.  VTEK is a cellular phone technology last I heard.  I think it's cute but like Eric said, it's still lacking torque. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||06-16-2000||08:54 PM||||Yeah, but if you have the displacement/cubes and that technology it would be awesome.  Bottom and top end power, whew.  Sounds dangerous in one of our little babys.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||06-16-2000||10:43 PM||||I think Lexus uses the same deal on their V8s but still only produce 300hp.  If you're into high tech gadgetry, VTEC is for you.  For the rest of us, we'll just settle for our mid 400 horsepower small blocks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
